<h3><strong>Key Noise Regulations</strong></h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>General Nuisance Threshold</strong>: A noise level that is <strong>10 dB over the ambient background level</strong> is considered a nuisance and is subject to enforcement.</li>
<li><strong>Waterways</strong>: Engine noise from boats, outboards, and amplifying systems must be kept to a minimum when near piers, floats, or yacht anchorages.</li>
<li><strong>Zoning Restrictions</strong>: Certain businesses, such as places of amusement or assembly, must be sufficiently sound-insulated to confine noise to the premises. Additionally, home occupations must not create noise that is discernible at any lot boundary.</li>
<li><strong>Construction Noise</strong>: While the town code has general nuisance provisions, common practice in Massachusetts towns often limits construction to daytime hours (typically 7:00 AM – 7:00 PM), though you should verify specific permit conditions with the <a href="https://www.weymouth.ma.us/DocumentCenter/View/3937/3454-SP-87-Wharf-St-19-172-2-7-14-21-Decision">Weymouth Building Department</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h3><strong>Typical Exemptions</strong></h3>
<p>Based on regional standards often adopted by the <a href="https://www.mahb.org/local-boh-regulations/noise/">Massachusetts Association of Health Boards</a>, the following are typically exempt from noise limits:</p>
