No, the rear differential on a Ford E-250 does <strong>not</strong> get fluid from the transmission fluid reservoir. The transmission and the rear differential are two separate systems that use different types of fluid and have independent fill points.</p>
<h3>Key Differences</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Fluid Types:</strong>
<ul>
<li><strong>Transmission:</strong> Uses Automatic Transmission Fluid (ATF), such as <strong>Motorcraft Mercon LV</strong> or <strong>Mercon V</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Rear Differential:</strong> Uses heavy-duty gear oil, typically <strong>SAE 75W-140</strong> or <strong>80W-90</strong> synthetic gear lubricant.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Independent Reservoirs:</strong>
<ul>
<li>The transmission has its own pan and a dipstick under the hood for checking levels.</li>
<li>The rear differential is a sealed housing on the rear axle. It has a dedicated <strong>fill plug</strong> located directly on the differential housing or cover.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Maintenance:</strong> Changing transmission fluid does not affect the differential. To service the differential, you must drain it by removing the rear cover or drain plug and then refill it through its own fill port.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Note:</strong> If your E-250 has a limited-slip differential, you may also need to add a <strong>friction modifier</strong> additive when refilling the differential fluid.</p>
