a car dealership has a car in the front of their lot and the price on the front windshield says $2 can I hold them legally accountable to sell me that car for only $2</p>
<p><strong>Search&#39;s response:</strong><br>
Likely <strong>no</strong>. While consumer protection laws generally require dealerships to honor advertised prices, a $2 price tag on a vehicle is almost certainly considered an <strong>obvious clerical error</strong> or &quot;mistake&quot; rather than a binding offer.</p>
<h3>The Legal Breakdown</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Invitation to Treat vs. Binding Offer</strong>: In contract law, a price tag or advertisement is typically considered an <strong>&quot;invitation to treat&quot;</strong>—an invitation for <em>you</em> to make an offer—rather than a binding contract. A contract is only formed when you offer to buy and the dealer accepts.</li>
<li><strong>Doctrine of Mistake</strong>: If a price is so low that a reasonable person would know it is a typo (e.g., $2 for a car worth $20,000), courts generally allow the seller to void the &quot;agreement&quot; because there was no &quot;meeting of the minds&quot;.</li>
<li><strong>Unfair and Deceptive Practices</strong>: Many states, including <a href="https://www.mass.gov/info-details/the-massachusetts-consumer-protection-law">Massachusetts</a>, prohibit dealerships from refusing to sell at an advertised price. However, these laws are designed to prevent &quot;bait and switch&quot; tactics where a dealer <em>intentionally</em> lures you in with a low price they never intended to honor.</li>
</ul>
<h3>What You Can Do</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Ask if it&#39;s a &quot;Loss Leader&quot;</strong>: Occasionally, dealers use a single specific car (a &quot;loss leader&quot;) at an extreme discount to drive traffic. If this is a legitimate promotion, they must sell that <em>specific</em> VIN at that price.</li>
<li><strong>File a Complaint</strong>: If you believe the dealer is consistently using &quot;mistakes&quot; to trick customers, you can file a complaint with your <a href="https://www.mass.gov/how-to/file-a-consumer-complaint">State Attorney General</a> or the <a href="https://www.bbb.org/">Better Business Bureau</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Negotiate</strong>: While they won&#39;t sell for $2, pointing out the error might give you leverage for a better-than-average deal as a gesture of goodwill.</li>
