<p>Here is how your &quot;intentional deception&quot; argument stacks up against current laws:</p>
<h3>1. The &quot;Bait and Switch&quot; Argument</h3>
<p>If you can prove the dealer put &quot;$2&quot; on the car specifically to lure you in with no intention of ever selling it for that amount, you are describing <strong>bait and switch</strong>, which is illegal in Massachusetts.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The Reality</strong>: Under <strong>940 CMR 5.02(6)</strong>, a dealer cannot refuse to sell a vehicle for the price they advertised.</li>
<li><strong>The Catch</strong>: This rule is designed to stop &quot;drip pricing&quot; (adding hidden fees later) or &quot;baiting&quot; (advertising a car that doesn&#39;t exist). It rarely forces a sale for a price that a &quot;reasonable person&quot; would recognize as a mistake.</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. The &quot;Loss Leader&quot; (Costco Chicken) Strategy</h3>
<p>Costco intentionally loses money on chickens to sell you other items. In the car world, these are called <strong>&quot;Loss Leaders&quot;</strong>.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Legal Requirement</strong>: If a dealer advertises a car at a ridiculously low price as a legitimate promotion, they <strong>must</strong> sell that specific vehicle (identified by its VIN or stock number) at that price to the first person who accepts.</li>
<li><strong>Your Argument</strong>: If the dealer claims it was a &quot;mistake&quot; but they have a history of these &quot;mistakes&quot; to get people onto the lot, you could argue they are violating the <a href="https://www.mass.gov/info-details/the-massachusetts-consumer-protection-law">Massachusetts Consumer Protection Law (Chapter 93A)</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>3. The &quot;Doctrine of Mistake&quot;</h3>
<p>This is the dealer&#39;s strongest defense. Courts generally hold that if a price is so low it&#39;s &quot;too good to be true,&quot; there was no <strong>&quot;meeting of the minds&quot;</strong>.</p>
<ul>
<li>If a $30,000 car is marked $2, a judge will likely see it as a clerical error (a typo for $20,000 or $28,000) rather than a binding offer.</li>
<li>Contracts based on an <strong>obvious unilateral mistake</strong> are usually voidable by the mistaken party.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Next Steps if You Want to Push Back</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Document Everything</strong>: Take a photo of the $2 sign and the car&#39;s VIN.</li>
<li><strong>Ask for the Manager</strong>: Ask if this is an advertised &quot;loss leader&quot; promotion. If they say it’s a mistake, ask to see where they have officially corrected the error (e.g., on their website or a posted notice).</li>
<li><strong>File a Report</strong>: If they refuse to sell but keep the $2 sign up to trick others, you can file a complaint with the <a href="https://www.mass.gov/how-to/file-a-consumer-complaint">Massachusetts Attorney General&#39;s Office</a>. While it won&#39;t get you a $2 car, it could lead to the dealer being fined for deceptive practices.</li>
</ul>
