<li><strong>Corrective Measures</strong>: To avoid liability for an error, the store must correct the misrepresentation using the same medium (e.g., changing the sign) once discovered.</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. Evidence of &quot;Bait and Switch&quot;</h3>
<p>By leaving the $2 price up for two weeks, the dealer may be engaging in a classic <strong>bait and switch</strong> under <a href="https://www.mass.gov/info-details/the-massachusetts-consumer-protection-law">Chapter 93A</a>:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The Bait</strong>: Advertising a price so low it&#39;s guaranteed to stop traffic ($2).</li>
<li><strong>The Switch</strong>: Refusing to sell at that price and trying to pivot you to a different, higher-priced vehicle or the &quot;actual&quot; price of that car.</li>
<li><strong>Intent</strong>: If you can prove they have turned away multiple people over those two weeks while keeping the sign up, you have strong evidence that the &quot;mistake&quot; is an intentional tool to generate leads.</li>
</ul>
<h3>3. Can You Force the Sale?</h3>
<p>Even with two weeks of evidence, <strong>forcing a $2 sale is extremely difficult.</strong></p>
