<li><strong>Unconscionability</strong>: Courts rarely enforce contracts where the terms are &quot;unconscionable&quot; or where there is no &quot;meeting of the minds&quot;. If the car is worth $20,000, a judge may rule that no reasonable person believed the dealer truly intended to sell it for $2.</li>
<li><strong>The Outcome</strong>: Rather than getting a $2 car, a successful legal challenge usually results in the dealer being <strong>fined</strong> or sued for damages for their deceptive practices.</li>
