<p><strong>Your prompt:</strong><br>
A colleague is arguing that this is rejected by the medical community that pandas exist.Is that skepticism true</p>
<p><strong>Search&#39;s response:</strong><br>
While it is incorrect to say the medical community &quot;rejects&quot; the existence of <strong>PANDAS</strong> or <strong>PANS</strong>, your colleague&#39;s skepticism reflects an ongoing and significant <strong>medical controversy</strong>.</p>
<p>The status of these conditions can be summarized as follows:</p>
<h3><strong>1. Institutional Recognition</strong></h3>
<p>PANDAS and PANS are recognized by major health and research organizations, though they are often viewed as &quot;hypotheses&quot; or &quot;clinical syndromes&quot; rather than universally established diseases.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>NIMH:</strong> The <a href="https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/pandas">National Institute of Mental Health</a> (NIMH) provides diagnostic criteria and supports ongoing research into the conditions.</li>
<li><strong>AAP:</strong> The <a href="https://publications.aap.org/pediatrics/article/155/3/e2024070334/200384/Pediatric-Acute-Onset-Neuropsychiatric-Syndrome">American Academy of Pediatrics</a> (AAP) recently released a clinical report acknowledging PANS as a &quot;likely valid diagnosis,&quot; though it urges a &quot;deliberate and cautious approach&quot; due to limited evidence.</li>
<li><strong>WHO:</strong> The <a href="https://icd.who.int/en">World Health Organization</a> included PANDAS in the <strong>ICD-11</strong> (the international standard for diagnostic codes), which is a major step toward global medical acceptance.</li>
</ul>
<h3><strong>2. Sources of Skepticism</strong></h3>
<p>The &quot;rejection&quot; your colleague mentions often stems from specific scientific gaps:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Lack of Biomarkers:</strong> There is currently <strong>no definitive lab or blood test</strong> to prove a child has PANDAS; it is a &quot;diagnosis of exclusion&quot; based on observed symptoms.</li>
<li><strong>Causality vs. Coincidence:</strong> Because strep infections are extremely common in school-aged children, many doctors argue that a child developing OCD after strep might just be a coincidence rather than a direct cause-and-effect relationship.</li>
<li><strong>Treatment Evidence:</strong> While some studies show success with antibiotics or immune therapy, critics point to a lack of large-scale, randomized controlled trials that definitively prove these treatments work better than standard psychiatric care.</li>
</ul>
<h3><strong>3. The Current Reality</strong></h3>
<p>In practice, the medical community is divided. Many pediatricians and neurologists at leading institutions—including <strong>Stanford</strong>, <strong>Harvard</strong>, and <strong>Columbia</strong>—have dedicated PANS/PANDAS clinics. However, many &quot;community&quot; doctors remain skeptical and may prefer to treat the symptoms as standard OCD or tic disorders rather than as an autoimmune condition.</p>
<p>In summary, the medical community generally agrees that some children experience a <strong>sudden, dramatic onset</strong> of neuropsychiatric symptoms, but they continue to debate exactly <strong>why</strong> it happens and <strong>how</strong> it should be treated.</p>
<p>Would you like to see the <strong>standard diagnostic criteria</strong> used by the NIMH to identify these cases?</p>
<br></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--6-col mdl-typography--body-1 mdl-typography--text-right"></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col mdl-typography--caption"><b>Products:</b><br>&emsp;AI Mode<br><b>Why is this here?</b><br>&emsp;This activity was saved to your Google Account because the following settings were on:&nbsp;Search Services History.&nbsp;You can control these settings &nbsp;<a href="https://myaccount.google.com/activitycontrols">here</a>.</div></div></div><div class="outer-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col mdl-shadow--2dp"><div class="mdl-grid"><div class="header-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col"><p class="mdl-typography--title">AI Mode<br></p></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--6-col mdl-typography--body-1">Searched for active active acting mechanism of nicotine<br>Mar 9, 2026, 2:57:28 PM EDT<br><p><strong>Your prompt:</strong><br>
active active acting mechanism of nicotine</p>
<p><strong>Search&#39;s response:</strong><br>
Nicotine primarily acts as an <strong>agonist</strong> at nicotinic acetylcholine receptors (<strong>nAChRs</strong>), which are ligand-gated ion channels widely distributed in the central and peripheral nervous systems. Its acting mechanism involves a complex cycle of <strong>activation</strong>, <strong>desensitization</strong>, and subsequent <strong>upregulation</strong>, which collectively drive its reinforcing effects and physical dependence.</p>
<h3>1. Primary Molecular Mechanism</h3>
<p>Nicotine mimics the endogenous neurotransmitter <strong>acetylcholine (ACh)</strong>.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Binding and Activation</strong>: Nicotine binds to the extracellular portion of nAChRs (most notably the <strong>α4β2</strong> and <strong>α7</strong> subtypes in the brain).</li>
<li><strong>Ion Influx</strong>: This binding opens the receptor&#39;s central pore, allowing cations—specifically <strong>sodium ($Na^+$)</strong> and <strong>calcium ($Ca^{2+}$)</strong>—to flow into the cell.</li>
<li><strong>Depolarization</strong>: The influx of these positive ions depolarizes the neuron, triggering an action potential and the release of various neurotransmitters.</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. Effects on the Reward Pathway</h3>
<p>The addictive nature of nicotine is largely due to its action on the <strong>mesolimbic dopamine system</strong>.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Dopamine Release</strong>: In the <strong>ventral tegmental area (VTA)</strong>, nicotine stimulates nAChRs on dopaminergic neurons, leading to increased dopamine release in the <strong>nucleus accumbens (NAc)</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Disinhibition</strong>: While nicotine initially activates inhibitory <strong>GABAergic</strong> neurons, these receptors desensitize rapidly. This leads to &quot;disinhibition,&quot; where the braking effect on dopamine neurons is removed, resulting in a prolonged dopamine surge.</li>
<li><strong>Glutamate Potentiation</strong>: Nicotine also acts on presynaptic <strong>α7</strong> receptors to increase the release of glutamate (an excitatory neurotransmitter), further exciting the dopamine neurons.</li>
</ul>
<h3>3. Receptor Desensitization and Upregulation</h3>
<p>Unlike acetylcholine, which is rapidly broken down by the enzyme <strong>acetylcholinesterase</strong>, nicotine remains in the synapse for a much longer period.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Desensitization</strong>: Persistent exposure causes nAChRs to enter a &quot;desensitized&quot; state—a closed, unresponsive conformation despite nicotine being bound.</li>
<li><strong>Upregulation</strong>: To compensate for this functional loss, the brain increases the total number (density) of nAChRs on the cell surface. This upregulation is a hallmark of chronic nicotine use and contributes to tolerance and withdrawal.</li>
</ul>
<h3>4. Peripheral and Other Central Effects</h3>
<p>Nicotine&#39;s reach extends beyond the reward system to other physiological processes:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Adrenal Glands</strong>: Stimulates the release of <strong>epinephrine (adrenaline)</strong> from the adrenal medulla, increasing heart rate, blood pressure, and blood glucose.</li>
<li><strong>Cognition</strong>: Enhances <strong>acetylcholine</strong> and <strong>norepinephrine</strong> release, which can improve attention, memory, and alertness.</li>
<li><strong>Aversion</strong>: High doses can activate a separate &quot;aversion&quot; pathway in the <strong>medial habenula</strong> and <strong>interpeduncular nucleus</strong>, which limits excessive intake.</li>
</ul>
<p>Would you like to explore how <strong>smoking cessation medications</strong> like <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3025154/">Varenicline (Chantix)</a> specifically target these receptor mechanisms to reduce cravings?</p>
<p><strong>Your prompt:</strong><br>
protective properties of neuro epinephrine and how it protects the heart when it has to make it beat faster</p>
<p><strong>Search&#39;s response:</strong><br>
Norepinephrine (noradrenaline) acts as a critical &quot;emergency&quot; signal that protects the heart by ensuring it can meet high energy demands while maintaining stable blood flow and pressure. When the heart must beat faster, norepinephrine coordinates a system-wide response to optimize efficiency and prevent cardiovascular collapse.</p>
<h3>1. Optimization of Blood Flow (Perfusion)</h3>
<p>One of the most vital protective roles of norepinephrine is maintaining <strong>coronary perfusion pressure</strong>.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Vasoconstriction</strong>: By stimulating $\alpha$-adrenergic receptors in peripheral blood vessels, it causes them to narrow, which raises the overall blood pressure.</li>
<li><strong>Restoring Pressure</strong>: This increase in pressure is essential for &quot;pushing&quot; oxygen-rich blood into the coronary arteries that supply the heart muscle itself. Without this, a fast-beating heart could quickly run out of oxygen (ischemia) and fail.</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. Increasing Cardiac Output Efficiency</h3>
<p>Norepinephrine doesn&#39;t just make the heart beat faster; it makes each beat more effective.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Enhanced Contractility</strong>: It stimulates $\beta_1$-adrenergic receptors, which increases the force of each contraction (an inotropic effect).</li>
<li><strong>Preload Management</strong>: It constricts veins, which helps &quot;refill the tank&quot; by pushing more blood back toward the heart, ensuring there is enough volume to pump out even at high speeds.</li>
</ul>
<h3>3. Metabolic and Cellular Protection</h3>
<p>When released in controlled amounts, norepinephrine helps the heart adapt to stress:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Energy Mobilization</strong>: It triggers the release of glucose (sugar) and the breakdown of fats, providing the heart with the immediate &quot;fuel&quot; it needs to sustain a higher workload.</li>
<li><strong>Myocyte Protection</strong>: Evidence suggests that small amounts of norepinephrine released directly within the heart can protect cardiac cells (myocytes) against injury from low oxygen levels.</li>
<li><strong>Angiogenesis</strong>: It can stimulate the production of growth factors like <strong>VEGF</strong>, which encourages the formation of new blood vessels to better support heart tissue.</li>
</ul>
<h3>4. Comparison to Epinephrine (Adrenaline)</h3>
<p>In clinical settings like <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9065839/">cardiogenic shock</a>, norepinephrine is often preferred over epinephrine because it is considered &quot;cleaner&quot; for the heart:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Lower Oxygen Cost</strong>: It is generally less thermogenic and may be more efficient regarding myocardial oxygen consumption.</li>
<li><strong>Reduced Arrhythmia Risk</strong>: It is often associated with a lower risk of dangerous irregular heartbeats (arrhythmias) compared to other stimulants like dopamine.</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<p><strong>Important Note</strong>: While norepinephrine is protective in acute stress, chronic exposure to high levels (such as in long-term heart failure) can be damaging, leading to <a href="https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/clc.4960211305">pathological remodeling</a> or scarring of the heart muscle.</p>
<p>Would you like to know more about how <strong>Beta-Blockers</strong> are used to counteract the long-term &quot;toxic&quot; effects of high norepinephrine levels?</p>
<br></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--6-col mdl-typography--body-1 mdl-typography--text-right"></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col mdl-typography--caption"><b>Products:</b><br>&emsp;AI Mode<br><b>Why is this here?</b><br>&emsp;This activity was saved to your Google Account because the following settings were on:&nbsp;Search Services History.&nbsp;You can control these settings &nbsp;<a href="https://myaccount.google.com/activitycontrols">here</a>.</div></div></div><div class="outer-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col mdl-shadow--2dp"><div class="mdl-grid"><div class="header-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col"><p class="mdl-typography--title">AI Mode<br></p></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--6-col mdl-typography--body-1">Searched for what country is the Federal Reserve based in<br>Mar 9, 2026, 12:34:58 PM EDT<br><p><strong>Your prompt:</strong><br>
what country is the Federal Reserve based in</p>
<p><strong>Search&#39;s response:</strong><br>
The Federal Reserve (often called &quot;the Fed&quot;) is based in the <strong>United States</strong>.</p>
<p>It serves as the country&#39;s central bank and is headquartered in <strong>Washington, D.C.</strong>, at the <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/aboutthefed/aroundtheboard/eccles.htm">Marriner S. Eccles Building</a>.</p>
<h3>Key Locations and Structure</h3>
<p>The Federal Reserve is not just a single bank but a system of multiple entities spread across the U.S.:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Board of Governors:</strong> The governing body located in <strong>Washington, D.C.</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>12 Regional Reserve Banks:</strong> These are located in major cities throughout the country to represent different geographic districts:
<ul>
<li>Boston, New York, Philadelphia, Cleveland, Richmond, Atlanta, Chicago, St. Louis, Minneapolis, Kansas City, Dallas, and San Francisco.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC):</strong> The policy-making group that typically meets in <strong>Washington, D.C.</strong> to set national monetary policy, such as interest rates.</li>
</ul>
<p>Would you like to know more about how the <strong>Board of Governors</strong> is chosen or how the <strong>12 regional districts</strong> are divided?</p>
<p><strong>Your prompt:</strong><br>
but the Federal Reserve I mean Washington DC technically isn&#39;t part of the US its sovereignty from the US</p>
<p><strong>Search&#39;s response:</strong><br>
Actually, <strong>Washington, D.C., is a sovereign part of the United States</strong>. While it is not part of any <em>state</em>, it is a federal district under the exclusive jurisdiction of the <a href="https://statehood.dc.gov/page/faq">U.S. Congress</a>.</p>
<p>The idea that D.C. is a separate, independent &quot;city-state&quot; is a common misconception, often linked to <a href="https://www.facebook.com/MansfieldWrites/posts/from-the-beginning-washington-dc-was-meant-to-be-different-not-a-state-not-behol/1314551470056841/">unverified theories</a> about the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/District_of_Columbia_Organic_Act_of_1871">District of Columbia Organic Act of 1871</a>. In reality, the legal status of the District is as follows:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Constitutional Basis:</strong> <a href="https://www.heritage.org/the-constitution/report/the-constitution-created-the-district-columbia-and-only-the-constitution">Article I, Section 8</a> of the Constitution specifically allows for a federal district to serve as the &quot;Seat of the Government&quot;.</li>
<li><strong>Federal Authority:</strong> It is governed by <a href="https://law.justia.com/district-of-columbia/">federal law</a>. While it has a local mayor and council, <a href="https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/research-reports/dc-statehood-explained">Congress</a> has the final say over its budget and laws.</li>
<li><strong>Citizenship:</strong> Residents of D.C. are <a href="https://statehood.dc.gov/page/faq">U.S. citizens</a>, pay federal income taxes, and vote in presidential elections (via the <a href="https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/R47101">23rd Amendment</a>).</li>
<li><strong>The Federal Reserve:</strong> The Fed&#39;s headquarters in D.C. is a <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/aboutthefed/fedexplained/who-we-are.htm">federal agency</a> created by an <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/faqs/about_14986.htm">Act of Congress</a> in 1913. It is &quot;independent within the government,&quot; meaning it makes its own policy decisions, but it is still <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/faqs/about_12799.htm">fully accountable</a> to the U.S. government.</li>
</ul>
<p>The confusion often stems from the fact that D.C. is <strong>neutral territory</strong>—meaning it doesn&#39;t belong to any state—to ensure the federal government isn&#39;t controlled by a single state&#39;s interests.</p>
<p><strong>Your prompt:</strong><br>
Why was the 1871 Act so important for D.C.?</p>
<p><strong>Search&#39;s response:</strong><br>
The <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/District_of_Columbia_Organic_Act_of_1871">District of Columbia Organic Act of 1871</a> was historically important because it <strong>unified the District&#39;s governance</strong> for the first time.</p>
<p>Before this act, the federal district was a patchwork of three separate local governments: the City of Washington, the City of Georgetown, and the rural Washington County.</p>
<h3>Key Impacts of the 1871 Act</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Unified Municipal Government:</strong> The Act repealed the individual charters of Washington and Georgetown, merging them into a single entity called the District of Columbia. This is why the terms &quot;Washington&quot; and &quot;D.C.&quot; are often used interchangeably today.</li>
<li><strong>Territorial Government:</strong> It established a new form of government modeled after U.S. territories, featuring a presidentially appointed governor and a bicameral legislature (an appointed upper house and an elected lower house).</li>
<li><strong>Modernization &amp; Infrastructure:</strong> The Act created a <strong>Board of Public Works</strong> to handle massive infrastructure projects like paving roads and building sewers, which were severely lacking after the Civil War.</li>
<li><strong>Bankruptcy &amp; Change in Control:</strong> Under Governor Alexander &quot;Boss&quot; Shepherd, the District underwent rapid modernization but also <a href="https://www.dcpolicycenter.org/publications/statehood-shifting-landscape/">went bankrupt</a> by spending three times its authorized budget. In response, Congress revoked the territorial government in 1874 and replaced it with a three-member commission, ending local self-rule for nearly a century until the <a href="https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF12577">1973 Home Rule Act</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Addressing the &quot;Corporate&quot; Myth</h3>
<p>The Act used the term <strong>&quot;municipal corporation&quot;</strong> to define the new government entity. In legal terms, this simply means the city was incorporated as a legal body that could enter contracts and sue/be sued—a standard practice for nearly every city in the U.S.. However, this phrasing is frequently <a href="https://www.politifact.com/factchecks/2024/feb/12/tiktok-posts/an-1871-law-did-not-make-the-united-states-governm/">misinterpreted in conspiracy theories</a> as having turned the entire United States into a private business corporation.</p>
<p>Would you like to explore how the <strong>1973 Home Rule Act</strong> finally restored the right for D.C. residents to elect their own <strong>Mayor and Council</strong>?</p>
