<p><strong>Search&#39;s response:</strong><br>
In legal and administrative terms, there is a big distinction between a <strong>private corporation</strong> (like Apple or Walmart) and a <strong>municipal corporation</strong> (like a city).</p>
<p>While they share the word &quot;corporation,&quot; their DNA is completely different:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The Goal:</strong> A private corporation exists to generate <strong>profit</strong> for its shareholders. A municipal corporation exists to provide <strong>public services</strong> (like police, water, and roads) to its residents.</li>
<li><strong>The &quot;Shareholders&quot;:</strong> In a city, the &quot;shareholders&quot; are the citizens. They don&#39;t get a dividend check; instead, they get services in exchange for taxes.</li>
<li><strong>Legal Status:</strong> &quot;Incorporating&quot; a city is just the legal process of giving it a &quot;body&quot; (the Latin root <em>corpus</em>). This allows the city to sign a contract to build a bridge or own a fleet of fire trucks without the individual politicians being personally liable for the debt.</li>
</ul>
<p>If a city isn&#39;t incorporated, it’s just a collection of people with no legal standing to manage a budget or enforce local laws. Almost every major city in the U.S.—from New York to a small town in Ohio—is technically a &quot;municipal corporation.&quot;</p>
<p>The tension you&#39;re pointing out usually comes from <strong>how</strong> a city is run. When a government starts prioritizing revenue (like heavy fines or selling off public utilities) over the well-being of the people, it can certainly feel like it&#39;s being run like a profit-hungry business.</p>
