<br></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--6-col mdl-typography--body-1 mdl-typography--text-right"></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col mdl-typography--caption"><b>Products:</b><br>&emsp;AI Mode<br><b>Why is this here?</b><br>&emsp;This activity was saved to your Google Account because the following settings were on:&nbsp;Search Services History.&nbsp;You can control these settings &nbsp;<a href="https://myaccount.google.com/activitycontrols">here</a>.</div></div></div><div class="outer-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col mdl-shadow--2dp"><div class="mdl-grid"><div class="header-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col"><p class="mdl-typography--title">AI Mode<br></p></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--6-col mdl-typography--body-1">Searched for how long have people been crushing cloves and smoking them in cigarettes<br>Mar 6, 2026, 11:56:19 AM EDT<br><p><strong>Your prompt:</strong><br>
how long have people been crushing cloves and smoking them in cigarettes</p>
<p><strong>Search&#39;s response:</strong><br>
People have been smoking crushed cloves in cigarettes for roughly <strong>145 years</strong>, though evidence suggests occasional use of cloves in tobacco mixtures as early as the <strong>17th century</strong>.</p>
<p>The formal practice and industry centered around clove cigarettes, known as <strong>kreteks</strong>, trace back to the following key periods:</p>
<h3><strong>Origins in Indonesia (1880s)</strong></h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Initial Invention</strong>: Clove cigarettes were created around <strong>1880</strong> by <strong>Haji Djamhari</strong> in Kudus, Central Java. Seeking relief from chest pains and asthma, Djamhari experimented by adding dried, crushed clove buds to his hand-rolled tobacco cigarettes.</li>
<li><strong>Medicinal Roots</strong>: They were originally sold in pharmacies as a &quot;medicinal cigarette&quot; (<em>rokok cengkeh</em>) for respiratory ailments before becoming a popular consumer product.</li>
<li><strong>Naming</strong>: The name &quot;kretek&quot; is an onomatopoeia for the distinctive <strong>crackling sound</strong> the burning cloves make when smoked.</li>
<li><strong>Commercial Growth</strong>: Serial production began in <strong>1906</strong> with the &quot;Tjap Bal Tiga&quot; brand, leading to a massive domestic industry that today accounts for roughly <strong>90%</strong> of the Indonesian cigarette market.</li>
</ul>
<h3><strong>Expansion to the West (1970s–1980s)</strong></h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>U.S. Introduction</strong>: While they were imported as early as <strong>1968</strong>, they did not gain significant traction in the United States until the <strong>mid-1970s</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>The 1980s &quot;Clove Craze&quot;</strong>: Kreteks became a major youth fad in the <strong>early 1980s</strong>, starting on the California coast before spreading inland.</li>
<li><strong>Regulatory Ban</strong>: In <strong>2009</strong>, the U.S. government banned flavored cigarettes, including cloves, citing their appeal to children. To bypass this, many manufacturers shifted to producing <strong>clove &quot;cigars,&quot;</strong> which use tobacco-based paper to remain legal.</li>
</ul>
<p>Would you like to know more about the <strong>health effects</strong> of clove cigarettes or the specific <strong>brands</strong> that are still available today?</p>
<p><strong>Your prompt:</strong><br>
