<p><strong>Search&#39;s response:</strong><br>
No, <strong>Marlboro 83s</strong> do not contain cloves; they are a variant of the standard <strong>Marlboro Red</strong> blend. While Philip Morris produces a specific &quot;Marlboro Clove&quot; product for certain international markets, it is distinct from the 83s line.</p>
<p>The ingredients and characteristics of Marlboro 83s are as follows:</p>
<h3><strong>Tobacco Blend and Ingredients</strong></h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Core Blend</strong>: According to retailers like Turks &amp; Caicos Shop, Marlboro Reds (including the 83s variant) use a precisely matched &quot;American Blend&quot; consisting of <strong>Virginia, Burley, and Oriental</strong> tobaccos.</li>
<li><strong>Additives</strong>: Common additives in this blend include <strong>sugars</strong> (sucrose or invert sugar), <strong>glycerol</strong>, and <strong>propylene glycol</strong> for moisture retention.</li>
<li><strong>Chemical Enhancements</strong>: Historical data and expert analysis from sources like Wikipedia note that Marlboro uses <strong>ammonia</strong> and <strong>diammonium phosphate</strong> to enhance nicotine delivery, which can contribute to a &quot;smoother&quot; or &quot;aromatic&quot; profile often mistaken for added spice.</li>
</ul>
<h3><strong>Product Specifications</strong></h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Dimensions</strong>: As noted in FDA technical reviews, the &quot;83&quot; in the name refers to the length of the cigarette in <strong>millimeters</strong> (83.0 mm), making it slightly longer than a standard King Size (typically 80 mm) but shorter than a 100s (98.5 mm).</li>
<li><strong>Flavor Profile</strong>: Reviewers on Influenster describe them as being identical to a &quot;really fresh Marlboro Red,&quot; offering a bold and full-bodied taste.</li>
<li><strong>Nicotine and Tar</strong>: They maintain a full-flavor profile similar to Marlboro Reds, which typically yield approximately <strong>1.1 mg to 1.2 mg</strong> of nicotine per cigarette.</li>
</ul>
<h3><strong>Distinction from Clove Cigarettes</strong></h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Characterizing Flavors</strong>: In the United States, the FDA&#39;s 2009 ban on characterizing flavors (except menthol) prohibits cigarettes from containing clove as a primary flavoring agent.</li>
<li><strong>Specialty Products</strong>: While &quot;Marlboro Clove&quot; exists as a specific aromatic product in markets like Indonesia, the standard <strong>Marlboro 83s King Box</strong> sold in the U.S. is strictly a non-flavored tobacco product.</li>
</ul>
<p>Are you experiencing a specific <strong>aftertaste or aroma</strong> from the 83s that reminds you of cloves?</p>
