<p><strong>Search&#39;s response:</strong><br>
It is highly unlikely that <strong>Marlboro 83s</strong> are processed on the same equipment as clove cigarettes due to strict federal laws and the separate nature of their manufacturing supply chains.</p>
<h3><strong>1. Federal Legal Bans</strong></h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>The 2009 Ban</strong>: The <a href="https://www.fda.gov/tobacco-products/rules-regulations-and-guidance/menthol-and-other-flavors-cigarettes">U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA)</a> prohibits the sale of cigarettes with &quot;characterizing flavors,&quot; including clove.</li>
<li><strong>Cross-Contamination</strong>: Because of this ban, a U.S. manufacturing facility producing a standard cigarette like Marlboro 83s would not be permitted to have raw clove material or clove-flavored tobacco on the same production floor, as even trace amounts could technically violate federal flavoring standards.</li>
</ul>
<h3><strong>2. Separate Manufacturing Entities</strong></h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Marlboro 83s (Domestic)</strong>: These are manufactured by <strong>Philip Morris USA</strong> (a subsidiary of Altria) at their primary plant in <strong>Richmond, Virginia</strong>. This facility is dedicated to the U.S. market, which does not allow clove cigarettes.</li>
<li><strong>Marlboro Cloves (International)</strong>: Clove-variant Marlboros are produced for international markets (like Indonesia) by <strong>Philip Morris International (PMI)</strong>, which has been a completely separate company from Philip Morris USA since 2008. These products are not made in the same factories or even by the same parent company as the 83s you find in the U.S.</li>
</ul>
<h3><strong>3. Why You Might Taste &quot;Clove&quot;</strong></h3>
<p>If you are detecting a spicy or clove-like note in Marlboro 83s, it is likely due to:</p>
<ul>
