An <strong>MC Number</strong> (Motor Carrier Number) represents a company&#39;s <strong>Operating Authority</strong>—the legal permission granted by the <a href="https://www.fmcsa.dot.gov/registration/get-mc-number-authority-operate">FMCSA</a> to transport regulated goods or passengers for hire across state lines.</p>
<p>While it&#39;s often confused with the <strong>USDOT Number</strong>, they serve different purposes:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>MC Number (Operating Authority)</strong>: This is about <strong>business permission</strong>. It identifies that a carrier has the right &quot;economic authority&quot; to be paid for hauling freight or people. It is required for <strong>for-hire</strong> interstate carriers and usually involves a $300 filing fee and proof of insurance.</li>
<li><strong>USDOT Number (Safety ID)</strong>: This is about <strong>safety tracking</strong>. It acts as a unique ID used by the government to monitor a company&#39;s safety records, inspections, and crash history.</li>
</ul>
<h3><strong>Who needs an MC Number?</strong></h3>
<p>Generally, a company needs an MC number if they:</p>
<ul>
<li>Transport <strong>passengers</strong> in interstate commerce for a fee.</li>
<li>Transport <strong>federally regulated commodities</strong> (like most consumer goods) owned by others across state lines.</li>
<li>Operate as a <strong>freight broker</strong> or forwarder.</li>
</ul>
