Can you explain how freestyle snowboarding is actually scored? Also, if someone goes for a really tough trick but wipes out, is there any way they can still get a high score?</p>
<p><strong>Search&#39;s response:</strong><br>
Freestyle snowboarding is scored using a 0–100 scale based on a &quot;best-run&quot; or &quot;combined-run&quot; format. Judges evaluate performance using the <strong>D.E.A.L.</strong> criteria, which breaks down every move into four key pillars:</p>
<h3>1. The D.E.A.L. Scoring Criteria</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Difficulty (D):</strong> The technical level of the trick. More spins (e.g., a 1620 vs. a 1080), more inversions (flips), and &quot;switch&quot; (riding backward) takeoffs or landings increase this score.</li>
<li><strong>Execution (E):</strong> How well the trick was performed. Judges look for clean, held grabs (not just a &quot;poke&quot;), stable body position in the air, and making hard tricks look effortless.</li>
<li><strong>Amplitude (A):</strong> The height and distance achieved. In halfpipe, this is how high you get above the lip; in Big Air and Slopestyle, it&#39;s about hitting the &quot;sweet spot&quot; of the landing.</li>
<li><strong>Landing (L):</strong> The final component. A perfect landing is &quot;bolted&quot; with no hand drags, butt checks, or &quot;reverts&quot; (the board continuing to spin after touching down).</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. Can you still score high if you wipe out?</h3>
<p>The short answer is <strong>no</strong>, you cannot get a competitive high score if you fall on your main trick.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Severe Deductions:</strong> A full wipeout is considered a major failure in <strong>Execution</strong> and <strong>Landing</strong>, which typically caps a run&#39;s score at a very low level (often below 30 or 40), regardless of how hard the trick was.</li>
<li><strong>Minor Mistakes:</strong> If a rider &quot;stomps&quot; a massive trick but has a slight &quot;hand drag&quot; or &quot;butt check&quot; (touching the snow briefly for balance), they will receive a significant deduction but may still score higher than a rider who did a much simpler trick perfectly.</li>
<li><strong>Overall Impression:</strong> In disciplines like Slopestyle, a fall on one feature might not &quot;zero out&quot; the entire run, but it will ruin the &quot;flow&quot; and &quot;composition&quot; score, which usually makes up 40% of the total.</li>
</ul>
<p>In most major competitions like the <a href="https://www.nbcolympics.com/news/snowboarding-101-what-know-about-olympic-slopestyle">Olympics</a> or <a href="https://www.espn.com/olympics/story/_/id/47707150/how-does-olympic-snowboarding-work-rules-scoring-more">X Games</a>, athletes get multiple runs, so a fall on the first try simply means they must rely on their subsequent attempts to stay in the running.</p>
<p>Would you like to know more about the <strong>specific types of grabs</strong> or <strong>spin directions</strong> that give riders the biggest difficulty boost?</p>
