The term you have coined, <strong>Abstractum Absurdum</strong>, perfectly captures a specific psychological phenomenon often referred to in logic and cognitive science as a <strong>fallacy of association</strong> or a <strong>false causal link</strong>.</p>
<p>In formal terms, your definition describes a specific type of <strong>Non Sequitur</strong> (Latin for &quot;it does not follow&quot;). This occurs when the conclusion (I don&#39;t have my debit card) does not logically flow from the premise (I forgot my driver&#39;s license), even if both items are usually kept in the same place.</p>
<h3>Key Logical Components of your Coined Term</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Abstractum (The Abstract):</strong> You are mentally abstracting the <em>category</em> of &quot;wallet items&quot; or &quot;essentials.&quot; Because one member of that category is missing, you abstractly conclude the entire category—or specific other members—must also be missing.</li>
<li><strong>Absurdum (The Absurd):</strong> The &quot;absurdity&quot; lies in the jump from a single localized fact to a broader, incorrect assumption that defies the physical reality of the situation (the card being in your pocket or a different slot).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Related Formal Concepts</h3>
<p>While <strong>Abstractum Absurdum</strong> is your unique coining, it aligns with several established logical fallacies:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Fallacy of Division:</strong> This is the error of assuming that what is true for the whole (the wallet is &quot;missing&quot; or &quot;incomplete&quot;) must be true for every part (the debit card must also be &quot;missing&quot;).</li>
<li><strong>Argument from Ignorance (Argumentum ad Ignorantiam):</strong> Specifically, the &quot;absence of evidence is not evidence of absence&quot; rule. Just because you haven&#39;t seen your card yet (absence of evidence), you fallaciously conclude it is gone (evidence of absence).</li>
<li><strong>False Cause (Post Hoc Ergo Propter Hoc):</strong> Mistakenly believing that because you realized one item is missing, that realization &quot;causes&quot; or confirms the absence of another unrelated item.</li>
</ul>
<p>Your term effectively describes the &quot;mental fog&quot; or <strong>cognitive bias</strong> where the brain over-generalizes a negative realization to create a broader, more stressful (and absurd) reality than what actually exists.</p>
