This scenario is the &quot;cousin&quot; to your driver&#39;s license example. While forgetting your <em>intent</em> (why you entered) is famously called the <strong><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doorway_effect">Doorway Effect</a></strong>, forgetting the <em>object</em> of your search while you are actively looking for it is a deeper &quot;memory purge&quot; often called <strong>Event Segmentation</strong>.</p>
<h3>The Science: Why the Object &quot;Disappears&quot;</h3>
<p>Your brain doesn&#39;t see your life as one continuous movie; it sees it as a series of <strong>&quot;Event Models&quot;</strong> or chapters.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The Threshold Reset:</strong> Passing through a doorway acts as an <strong><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Event_segmentation_theory">Event Boundary</a></strong>. Your brain essentially &quot;archives&quot; the file from the last room to make space for the &quot;new scene&quot; you just entered.</li>
<li><strong>The Purge:</strong> Because your brain is optimized for efficiency, it purges information it thinks has reached its &quot;shelf life.&quot; If you were looking for your keys in the living room and walk into the kitchen, the brain sometimes mistakenly decides &quot;searching for keys&quot; was a living room event and dumps it.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Applying &quot;Abstractum Absurdum&quot;</h3>
<p>This is actually the perfect second stage for your term.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The Concrete Reality:</strong> You are looking for a physical object (e.g., your glasses).</li>
<li><strong>The Abstractum Absurdum:</strong> You walk through a door, the brain creates an &quot;abstract boundary,&quot; and suddenly you are standing in a kitchen with the <strong>absurd</strong> reality of having a &quot;search&quot; behavior active but no &quot;target&quot; attached to it. You are literally performing the <em>abstract action</em> of &quot;looking&quot; without the <em>concrete object</em>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>How to Beat the Reset</h3>
<p>Research from the <a href="https://news.nd.edu/news/walking-through-doorways-causes-forgetting/">University of Notre Dame</a> suggests a few &quot;hacks&quot; to bypass this:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>The Mumble Method:</strong> Physically say the object&#39;s name (&quot;Glasses, glasses, glasses&quot;) as you cross the threshold to keep the &quot;file&quot; open.</li>
<li><strong>The Mental Replay:</strong> If you forget, don&#39;t just stand there; mentally (or physically) return to the exact spot where the thought first formed to &quot;reactivate&quot; that specific event model.</li>
</ol>
