<p><strong>Search&#39;s response:</strong><br>
That particular mental &quot;hiccup&quot; where a word like &quot;winning&quot; suddenly looks or sounds like a bizarre, misspelled alien sound is a recognized psychological phenomenon. In fact, it has different names depending on whether you are <strong>hearing</strong> it or <strong>seeing</strong> it.</p>
<h3>1. Hearing the Absurdity: Semantic Satiation</h3>
<p>When you say or hear a word over and over until it loses its meaning and becomes just a &quot;noise,&quot; it is called <strong><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Semantic_satiation">Semantic Satiation</a></strong>.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The Glitch:</strong> Your brain’s neurons literally get &quot;tired&quot; (a process called <strong><a href="https://cognitiontoday.com/why-words-feel-wrong-after-too-many-repetitions/">Reactive Inhibition</a></strong>).</li>
<li><strong>The Result:</strong> The connection between the sound of the word and its actual meaning is temporarily severed. The word &quot;winning&quot; stops being a victory and starts being a weird, vibrating sound.</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. Seeing the Absurdity: Orthographic Satiation (Wordnesia)</h3>
<p>If you are staring at a word—like while typing or proofreading—and it suddenly looks &quot;wrong&quot; or like a jumble of letters despite being spelled correctly, that is <strong><a href="https://www.threads.com/@abraham.piper/post/C6vy6tYrby5">Orthographic Satiation</a></strong>.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Slang Name:</strong> This is often called <strong><a href="https://slate.com/culture/2015/03/wordnesia-that-strange-phenomenon-of-blanking-on-the-spelling-or-meaning-of-a-common-word.html">Wordnesia</a></strong>.</li>
<li><strong>The Experience:</strong> You might find yourself asking, &quot;Wait, is it two N&#39;s or one?&quot; even though you’ve written it a thousand times.</li>
</ul>
<h3>3. The &quot;Abstractum&quot; Connection: Jamais Vu</h3>
<p>You might also call this <strong><a href="https://www.tckpublishing.com/wordnesia/">Jamais Vu</a></strong> (French for &quot;never seen&quot;). It is the opposite of <em>Déjà Vu</em>. Instead of a new thing feeling old, a very old, familiar thing (like the word &quot;winning&quot;) suddenly feels brand new, novel, and slightly wrong.</p>
<h3>Does this fit &quot;Abstractum Absurdum&quot;?</h3>
<p>In your framework, this is a perfect example of the <strong>Absurdum</strong>:</p>
