Glenn Beck made his comments about Barack Obama during a guest appearance on the morning show <em><strong>Fox &amp; Friends</strong></em> on <strong>July 28, 2009</strong>.</p>
<p>During this appearance, Beck stated that President Obama had &quot;a deep-seated hatred for white people or the white culture&quot; and called him &quot;a racist&quot;. These remarks were made during a discussion regarding Obama&#39;s reaction to the arrest of Harvard professor Henry Louis Gates Jr. by a white police officer.</p>
<h3>Event Details and Content</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>The Specific Comment:</strong> Beck claimed that Obama had &quot;exposed himself as a guy—over and over and over again—who has a deep-seated hatred for white people, or the white culture&quot;.</li>
<li><strong>Context of the Discussion:</strong> The segment focused on Obama&#39;s comment that the police had &quot;acted stupidly&quot; in the arrest of Professor Gates. Beck argued that the President&#39;s default position was &quot;to see white people as typically unfair&quot;.</li>
<li><strong>On-Air Pushback:</strong> <em>Fox &amp; Friends</em> co-host Brian Kilmeade challenged the statement, noting that many of Obama&#39;s staffers, such as David Axelrod and Rahm Emanuel, were white. Beck responded by saying, &quot;I&#39;m not saying that he doesn&#39;t like white people. I&#39;m saying he has a problem. This guy is, I believe, a racist&quot;.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Viewing the Footage</h3>
<p>While full archives of daily morning shows from 2009 are not typically hosted on official network websites, you can find clips of the specific segment through the following sources:</p>
<ul>
