<li><strong>Historical Clips:</strong> Segments of the interview are often included in retrospectives of Beck&#39;s career or news reports regarding his departure from Fox News on platforms like <strong><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1_bOmMTIfWM">YouTube</a></strong>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Aftermath and Later Statements</h3>
<p>The comments led to an advertising boycott organized by ColorOfChange, eventually resulting in more than <strong>400 advertisers</strong> requesting that their commercials not run during Beck&#39;s program.</p>
<p>Over the years, Beck&#39;s stance on these comments has shifted:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>2010:</strong> He told Chris Wallace on <em>Fox News Sunday</em> that the comment &quot;shouldn&#39;t have been said&quot; and was &quot;not accurate&quot;.</li>
<li><strong>2016:</strong> He expressed regret to <em>The New Yorker</em>, stating that Obama had &quot;made [him] a better man&quot; by forcing him to listen to experiences he could not relate to.</li>
<li><strong>2021:</strong> During an appearance on <em>Tucker Carlson Tonight</em>, Beck <strong>retracted his previous apologies</strong>, stating, &quot;I take my apology back. I was exactly right and I even stated it right&quot;.</li>
</ul>
