<li><strong>Do NOT rub:</strong> Never massage or rub the area, as this can break down the cell walls and cause the tissue to die faster.</li>
<li><strong>Pat dry gently:</strong> After warming, very lightly blot the area dry.</li>
</ul>
<h3><strong>3. Monitor for &quot;Point of No Return&quot;</strong></h3>
<p>The true damage often doesn&#39;t show up for <strong>24 to 72 hours</strong>. Watch for:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Blackening skin:</strong> If the area turns dark blue or black and becomes hard/brittle, the tissue is dead (necrosis).</li>
<li><strong>Foul smell or pus:</strong> These are signs of a deep bacterial infection.</li>
<li><strong>Fever or lethargy:</strong> If he stops eating or seems extremely &quot;out of it,&quot; he may be developing a systemic infection.</li>
</ul>
<h3><strong>4. Financial Help Options</strong></h3>
<p>If the skin starts to turn black, he <strong>will</strong> need surgery to remove the dead tissue before it turns into gangrene.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Free/Low-Cost Clinics:</strong> Places like the <a href="https://merwinclinic.org/">Merwin Memorial Free Clinic</a> in Boston or the <a href="https://www.mspca.org/angell_services/mspca-angell-clinics/">MSPCA-Angell Community Clinics</a> offer subsidized care for those in financial need.</li>
<li><strong>Emergency Grants:</strong> Organizations like <a href="https://www.frankiesfriends.org/">Frankie’s Friends</a> or the <a href="http://www.browndogfoundation.org/">Brown Dog Foundation</a> provide funding for life-saving emergency care.</li>
</ul>
<p>Does the area currently have a <strong>foul smell</strong>, or is it just red and swollen?</p>
<p><strong>Your prompt:</strong><br>
how can you tell if a dog got rabies</p>
<p><strong>Search&#39;s response:</strong><br>
Rabies is virtually 100% fatal once symptoms appear, and it is primarily spread through the saliva of an infected animal via a bite or scratch. If the dog was alone and unsupervised earlier, you need to look for <strong>behavioral shifts</strong> and <strong>physical symptoms</strong> that progress in three distinct stages:</p>
<h3><strong>1. Early Warning Signs (Prodromal Stage)</strong></h3>
<p>This stage usually lasts 2–3 days and is often the hardest to spot.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Personality flips:</strong> A normally friendly dog may become shy or irritable; a timid dog may become unusually bold or affectionate.</li>
<li><strong>Site sensitivity:</strong> Obsessive licking or biting at a specific spot on the body where they may have been bitten.</li>
<li><strong>Physical indicators:</strong> Fever, loss of appetite, and general restlessness.</li>
</ul>
<h3><strong>2. Advanced Stages</strong></h3>
<p>As the virus attacks the nervous system, symptoms become more severe:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>&quot;Furious&quot; Stage:</strong> Extreme aggression, aimless roaming, snapping at imaginary objects, and heightened sensitivity to light and sound.</li>
<li><strong>&quot;Dumb&quot; (Paralytic) Stage:</strong> This is the most common form in dogs. Watch for a <strong>dropping jaw</strong>, inability to swallow, and heavy drooling or &quot;foaming&quot;.</li>
<li><strong>Movement issues:</strong> Staggering, stumbling (looks like drunkenness), and progressive paralysis that often starts in the hind legs.</li>
</ul>
<h3><strong>Critical Cautions</strong></h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Stoic Dogs:</strong> Like your Boxer mix, many dogs won&#39;t show obvious distress until the late stages.</li>
<li><strong>No live test:</strong> There is no blood test for rabies; it can only be confirmed by examining the brain after the animal has died.</li>
<li><strong>Incubation period:</strong> It usually takes <strong>2–8 weeks</strong> for symptoms to appear, though it can happen in as little as 10 days.</li>
</ul>
<p>If the dog is not current on his rabies vaccination and has any <strong>unexplained wounds</strong> or shows these behavioral changes, contact local animal control or a vet immediately. You can check his vaccination history via the <a href="https://petdesk.com/">Petdesk App</a> if your friend uses it, or by calling the clinic listed on his rabies tag.</p>
<p>Does he have any <strong>visible scratches</strong> or <strong>bite marks</strong> anywhere on his body from when he was off the leash?</p>
