Correct. If he was only out for a day and is acting weird <em>immediately</em> after, it&#39;s almost certainly <strong>not rabies</strong>.</p>
<p>The rabies virus has an <strong>incubation period</strong>, meaning it takes time—usually <strong>3 to 8 weeks</strong>—to travel from the bite site to the brain before it causes any behavior changes. It is physically impossible for a dog to catch rabies and show symptoms the very next day.</p>
<h3><strong>Why he&#39;s actually acting &quot;weird&quot;</strong></h3>
<p>Given the frostbite on his scrotum, his &quot;weird&quot; behavior is likely a reaction to <strong>pain and shock</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Pain Response:</strong> Even stoic dogs like Boxers get quiet, restless, or &quot;spacey&quot; when they are hurting.</li>
<li><strong>Adrenaline Crash:</strong> After the stress of being loose and the pain of freezing/thawing, he’s likely exhausted.</li>
<li><strong>Tissue Damage:</strong> The rewarming process for frostbite is notoriously painful and can make a dog act lethargic or irritable.</li>
</ul>
<h3><strong>What to do now</strong></h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Check for Wounds:</strong> Look for any actual bite marks or scratches. If he was bitten, he needs a rabies booster shot within 96 hours to be safe, regardless of his current behavior.</li>
<li><strong>Verify Vaccines:</strong> Check the <a href="https://www.rabiesaware.org/">Rabies Laws by State</a> to see the requirements in your area. If he’s up to date, you have even less to worry about.</li>
<li><strong>Monitor the Frostbite:</strong> If the &quot;weird&quot; behavior includes him not eating or becoming extremely weak, the frostbitten tissue might be getting infected.</li>
