<p><strong>Search&#39;s response:</strong><br>
It sounds like your dog might be experiencing the &quot;thawing&quot; stage of frostbite. The initial <strong>purple</strong> or <strong>blue</strong> color you saw usually indicates restricted blood flow from the cold. As the tissue warms up, it often transitions to <strong>red and swollen</strong>, which may be why he looks &quot;less purple&quot; now.</p>
<p>The scrotum is highly susceptible to frostbite because it has minimal hair and is far from the heart.</p>
<h3>Immediate Steps</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Do Not Rub:</strong> Avoid massaging or rubbing the area, as this can cause permanent tissue damage.</li>
<li><strong>Gently Rewarm:</strong> Use lukewarm water (ideally <strong>100°F–108°F</strong>) to gently soak or apply a compress to the area for about 20 minutes. If the water feels hot to your hand, it is too hot for his skin.</li>
<li><strong>Prevent Licking:</strong> Use an <strong>Elizabethan collar (cone)</strong> to stop him from licking the area, which can lead to infection.</li>
<li><strong>No Direct Heat:</strong> Never use a hairdryer or heating pad directly on the skin, as frostbitten tissue burns easily.</li>
</ul>
<h3>When to See a Vet</h3>
<p>Frostbite damage can take <strong>several days</strong> to fully appear. You should have him evaluated by a veterinarian at <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/frostbite-in-dogs">VCA Animal Hospitals</a> or an <a href="https://animalemergencycare.net/aecprevents/managing-frostbite-in-pets/">emergency clinic</a> immediately if you notice:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Blackened Skin:</strong> This is a sign of necrosis (dead tissue) and is a medical emergency.</li>
<li><strong>Blistering or Ulcers:</strong> These indicate a more severe second-degree frostbite.</li>
<li><strong>Lethargy or Shivering:</strong> These could be signs of concurrent <a href="https://news.orvis.com/dogs/recognize-signs-frostbite-hypothermia-dogs">hypothermia</a>.</li>
</ul>
