It’s a heart-stopping moment, but staying calm is your best tool for getting them back safely. Here is what you should do immediately:</p>
<h3><strong>If You Can See Them</strong></h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Stop Chasing:</strong> Most dogs think chasing is a game or get scared and run further.</li>
<li><strong>Run Away from Them:</strong> Turn and run in the opposite direction while calling their name happily. This triggers their instinct to &quot;chase&quot; you back.</li>
<li><strong>Drop to the Ground:</strong> Lie down or sit and act like you’ve found something incredibly interesting. Curiosity often brings them back to investigate.</li>
<li><strong>Use High-Value Rewards:</strong> If you have treats or a squeaky toy, use them to lure them in once they are close.</li>
</ul>
<h3><strong>If They Are Out of Sight</strong></h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Search the Immediate Area:</strong> Check hiding spots like under cars, in bushes, or behind buildings.</li>
<li><strong>Leave Out Familiar Scents:</strong> Place their bed, your unwashed clothes, or high-smell food (like canned food) outside your door to help them scent their way home.</li>
<li><strong>Report the Loss:</strong> Contact local animal control, shelters, and vet clinics within a 60-mile radius.</li>
<li><strong>Use Social Media:</strong> Post on <a href="https://nextdoor.com/">Nextdoor</a>, local Facebook &quot;Lost and Found&quot; groups, and <a href="https://www.pawboost.com/">PawBoost</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Create Flyers:</strong> Make bright, eye-catching posters with a clear photo, your phone number, and any reward details.</li>
</ul>
<h3><strong>Post-Recovery Tips</strong></h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Check the Microchip:</strong> Ensure your contact information is up-to-date with your chip provider.</li>
<li><strong>Training:</strong> Practice the &quot;Recall&quot; (Come) command in a fenced area using high-value rewards.</li>
<li><strong>GPS Tracking:</strong> Consider a tracking collar like an <a href="https://www.apple.com/airtag/">Apple AirTag</a> or a dedicated pet GPS tracker for the future.</li>
</ul>
