<p><strong>Your prompt:</strong><br>
I&#39;m trying to understand the 34.8% chance of still having not guessed it right after 10 guesses</p>
<p><strong>Search&#39;s response:</strong></p>
<h3>Understanding the 34.8% Chance of Failure</h3>
<p>The <strong>34.8% chance</strong> represents the probability of guessing incorrectly <strong>10 times in a row</strong> when there is a 1 in 10 chance of being right each time. This occurs because each guess is an <strong>independent event</strong>; failing one guess does not make you more likely to win the next one.</p>
<hr>
<h3>Step 1: Probability of a Single Failure</h3>
<p>If there are 10 numbers and only one is correct, the probability of being <strong>wrong</strong> on any single guess is:
$$P(\text{wrong}) = \frac{9}{10} = 0.9$$</p>
<hr>
<h3>Step 2: Probability of Consecutive Failures</h3>
<p>Because the target number changes every time and the incorrect numbers are not removed, the odds do not improve with each turn. To find the probability of multiple independent events happening together, you <strong>multiply</strong> the individual probabilities.
For 10 consecutive failures ($n = 10$), the calculation is:
$$(0.9) \times (0.9) \times (0.9) \times (0.9) \times (0.9) \times (0.9) \times (0.9) \times (0.9) \times (0.9) \times (0.9)$$
This is expressed as:
$$P(\text{fail 10 times}) = (0.9)^{10}$$</p>
<hr>
<h3>Step 3: Final Calculation</h3>
<p>Using the power of 10:
$$(0.9)^{10} \approx \mathbf{0.348678}$$
This result, approximately <strong>34.87%</strong>, confirms that even after 10 guesses, you still have more than a 1-in-3 chance of never having picked the correct number.</p>
<hr>
<h3><strong>Answer:</strong></h3>
<p>The <strong>34.8%</strong> chance of failure after 10 guesses comes from the formula $(0.9)^{10}$. Because the &quot;correct&quot; number is reset every time, you have a <strong>90%</strong> chance of being wrong for every single guess. Multiplying that 90% chance by itself ten times results in a <strong>34.87%</strong> probability of an unbroken losing streak.</p>
<p><strong>Your prompt:</strong><br>
how does one know to use the chance of being wrong versus the chance of being right</p>
<p><strong>Search&#39;s response:</strong><br>
To determine whether to use the chance of being <strong>wrong</strong> ($0.9$) or <strong>right</strong> ($0.1$), you look at whether your goal is to calculate a &quot;streak&quot; of specific outcomes or the overall chance of something happening &quot;at least once&quot;.</p>
<h3>1. Use the Chance of Being Wrong ($0.9$)</h3>
<p>Use the probability of failure when you need to find the odds of a <strong>perfect streak of misses</strong>.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The Math:</strong> You multiply the chance of failure for every attempt: $(0.9) \times (0.9) \times (0.9) \dots$.</li>
<li><strong>The Logic:</strong> If you want to know the chance that you have <strong>not</strong> guessed it right after 10 tries, every single one of those 10 tries <em>must</em> have been a failure. There is only one way for this to happen: fail, then fail, then fail, and so on.</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. Use the Chance of Being Right ($0.1$)</h3>
<p>Use the probability of success when you want to find the odds of <strong>winning on a specific turn</strong> or <strong>winning on every turn</strong>.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Specific Turn:</strong> The chance of winning on exactly the 4th turn is $0.1$.</li>
<li><strong>Perfect Winning Streak:</strong> The chance of winning 10 times in a row is $(0.1)^{10}$, which is $0.0000000001$.</li>
</ul>
<h3>3. The Shortcut: The &quot;Complement Rule&quot;</h3>
<p>In math, it is often easier to calculate the probability of &quot;at least one success&quot; by first finding the probability of <strong>zero successes</strong> and subtracting it from 1.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Question:</strong> &quot;What is the chance I get it right at least once in 10 tries?&quot;</li>
<li><strong>Step 1:</strong> Find the chance of failing every time: $(0.9)^{10} = 34.8%$.</li>
<li><strong>Step 2:</strong> Subtract that from $100%$ to get your answer: $1 - 0.348 = 65.2%$.</li>
</ul>
