<p>Here is why executing a high volume of trades doesn&#39;t automatically equate to massive returns for a beginner with $1,000:</p>
<h3>1. The Margins are Pennies, Not Dollars</h3>
<p>The core issue is competition. High-frequency trading firms spot arbitrage opportunities in milliseconds and exploit them instantly. What is left for a retail bot are margins that are often <strong>0.05% to 0.2%</strong> per trade.</p>
<ul>
<li>A $1,000 trade at a 0.1% margin makes you exactly <strong>$1.00</strong> in profit.</li>
<li>If you manage to execute 1,000 such trades in a month (which is a lot for a beginner&#39;s bot given liquidity constraints), you make $1,000 total in gross profit.</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. Transaction Fees Kill the Profits</h3>
<p>That $1,000 gross profit instantly gets eaten by exchange fees. Most exchanges charge around 0.1% per trade (both when buying and selling).</p>
<ul>
<li>Buying costs you $1.00 in fees. Selling costs you $1.00 in fees. Your $1.00 <em>profit</em> is completely wiped out by $2.00 in fees.</li>
<li><strong>Net Profit: -$1.00</strong></li>
</ul>
<p>This is why experienced traders use <strong>market maker</strong> or <strong>taker/maker</strong> fees carefully. A beginner often pays &quot;taker&quot; fees which make pure arbitrage very difficult to be profitable at small scales.</p>
<h3>3. Liquidity Bottlenecks</h3>
<p>You can&#39;t just trade your $1,000 back and forth infinitely fast. You are limited by how much volume is available at the slightly different prices you need. With only $1,000, you run into liquidity issues quickly, meaning you can&#39;t always execute a trade when an opportunity appears.</p>
<h3>Summary: Volume vs. Capital</h3>
<p>The misconception is that &quot;high volume&quot; equals &quot;high returns.&quot; In arbitrage, &quot;high volume&quot; means executing your <em>entire capital</em> many times over.</p>
<ul>
<li>A firm with <strong>$1 Million</strong> can make $1,000 per trade at 0.1% margin.</li>
<li>You with <strong>$1,000</strong> can make $1 per trade at 0.1% margin.</li>
</ul>
<p>To make serious money in arbitrage, you need either <strong>massive capital</strong> (to make the pennies add up to dollars quickly) or <strong>exclusive access</strong> to exchanges that major firms aren&#39;t monitoring yet.</p>
