<p><strong>Your prompt:</strong><br>
no no no no no you can use a guy frames and stuff to pull the data and then do calculations on the data inside of the iframes</p>
<p><strong>Search&#39;s response:</strong><br>
In 2026, using <strong>iframes</strong> to scrape and calculate data directly on a website is technically difficult due to browser security policies, but there is a &quot;scrappy&quot; way to do it on a local Windows box using specialized tools.</p>
<h3>1. The Challenge: Cross-Origin Security</h3>
<p>Modern browsers (Chrome, Edge) use <strong>Same-Origin Policy</strong> to block one website (your dashboard) from reading data inside an iframe from another website (e.g., Binance or Kraken). If you try to use standard JavaScript to &quot;pull&quot; price data out of a third-party iframe, the browser will block the request to prevent data theft.</p>
<h3>2. The &quot;Scrappy&quot; 2026 Workaround: Local Browser Automation</h3>
<p>Instead of a standard website, you use <strong>browser automation</strong> on your Windows box. This allows you to treat the browser like a &quot;user&quot; that can see and scrape everything inside those frames.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Software to use:</strong> <strong><a href="https://pptr.dev/">Puppeteer</a></strong> or <strong><a href="https://playwright.dev/">Playwright</a></strong>. These are free, open-source libraries that let you write a script to open a browser window, find the iframes, and extract the text inside them.</li>
<li><strong>The Workflow:</strong>
<ol>
<li>Your script opens the exchange page in a &quot;headless&quot; (invisible) browser.</li>
<li>It waits for the iframe to load.</li>
<li>It pulls the &quot;Ask&quot; price and &quot;Bid&quot; price from the frames.</li>
<li>It does the math: <code>(Bid - Ask) - Fees</code>.</li>
<li>It pushes that result to your HTML dashboard.</li>
</ol>
</li>
</ul>
<h3>3. DIY Implementation for your Windows Box</h3>
<p>You can write a simple Python script using <code>Playwright</code> to bypass iframe restrictions locally:</p>
<pre><code>import asyncio
from playwright.async_api import async_playwright

async def get_price():
    async with async_playwright() as p:
        browser = await p.chromium.launch(headless=True)
        page = await browser.new_page()
        await page.goto(&quot;https://exchange-website.com&quot;)
        
        # In 2026, you can specifically target a frame by name or URL
        frame = page.frame(name=&quot;price-widget&quot;) 
        price_text = await frame.inner_text(&quot;.current-price-selector&quot;)
        
        print(f&quot;Captured Price: {price_text}&quot;)
        await browser.close()

asyncio.run(get_price())
</code></pre>
