<li><strong>Visual Monitoring</strong>: You can set your dashboard to actually show the live iframes side-by-side so you can manually verify the &quot;gap&quot; before the bot executes.</li>
<li><strong>Safety</strong>: Since this is running on your <strong>old Windows box</strong> locally, you don&#39;t have to worry about a web-hosting provider banning your &quot;scraping&quot; activity.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Your prompt:</strong><br>
there&#39;s got to be a way to use JavaScript to simultaneously poll</p>
<p><strong>Search&#39;s response:</strong><br>
Yes, you can use JavaScript to simultaneously poll data from multiple sources. The correct modern technique is to use <strong><code>Promise.all()</code></strong> with the <strong><code>fetch()</code></strong> API.</p>
<p>This method allows you to fire off requests to multiple exchanges at the same time and calculate the arbitrage opportunities right there on your local Windows machine.</p>
<h3>The JavaScript Implementation</h3>
<p>This script demonstrates how to simultaneously poll two different (conceptual) exchanges and calculate a potential gap.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html lang=&quot;en&quot;&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;JS Simultaneous Polling Arbitrage&lt;/title&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
    &lt;h1&gt;Live Arbitrage Opportunities&lt;/h1&gt;
    &lt;p id=&quot;status&quot;&gt;Polling for data...&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p id=&quot;opportunity&quot;&gt;No opportunity found yet.&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;script&gt;
        // Use a proxy server on your Windows box to bypass browser CORS restrictions locally
        const PROXY = &#39;http://localhost:8080/&#39;; 
        const API_A = &#39;https://api.exchangeA.com/price/BTCUSD&#39;;
        const API_B = &#39;https://api.exchangeB.com/price/BTCUSD&#39;;

        async function pollPrices() {
            try {
                // Use Promise.all() to run fetch requests at the same time
                const [responseA, responseB] = await Promise.all([
                    fetch(PROXY + API_A),
                    fetch(PROXY + API_B)
                ]);

                const dataA = await responseA.json();
                const dataB = await responseB.json();

                // Assuming the APIs return data like { &quot;price&quot;: 60000.12 }
                const priceA = parseFloat(dataA.price);
                const priceB = parseFloat(dataB.price);

                calculateArbitrage(priceA, priceB);

            } catch (error) {
                document.getElementById(&#39;status&#39;).innerText = &#39;Error fetching data. Check your local proxy server.&#39;;
                console.error(error);
            }
        }

        function calculateArbitrage(pA, pB) {
            const gap = ((Math.abs(pA - pB) / Math.min(pA, pB)) * 100).toFixed(4);
            const statusEl = document.getElementById(&#39;opportunity&#39;);

            if (gap &gt; 0.1) { // Set your minimum profitable gap here
                statusEl.innerText = `OPPORTUNITY FOUND! Price Gap: ${gap}%`;
                statusEl.style.color = &#39;green&#39;;
                // This is where you would trigger your Python script to execute a trade
            } else {
                statusEl.innerText = `Gap: ${gap}% - Waiting for better opportunity.`;
                statusEl.style.color = &#39;white&#39;;
            }
        }

        // Poll every 3 seconds (adjust as needed to avoid API limits)
        setInterval(pollPrices, 3000); 
        pollPrices(); // Run immediately on load
    &lt;/script&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
</code></pre>
