In 2026, using the <strong>InterPlanetary File System (IPFS)</strong> to free up phone space involves offloading local data to the decentralized network and then deleting the local originals. Since running a full IPFS node directly on a phone is resource-intensive, the most efficient method is using <strong>IPFS-based storage services</strong>.</p>
<h3>1. Upload Files to IPFS</h3>
<p>To safely remove files from your phone, you must ensure they are &quot;pinned&quot; on the IPFS network so they don&#39;t disappear when your local cache is cleared.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Use a Mobile-Friendly Gateway or App:</strong> In 2026, services like <strong>Filebase</strong> or <strong>Pinata</strong> provide mobile-accessible web interfaces to upload your photos, videos, and large documents.</li>
<li><strong>Pin Your Data:</strong> Once uploaded, ensure the file is <strong>pinned</strong>. Pinning tells the network to keep the data permanently available even if your phone&#39;s temporary cache is deleted.</li>
<li><strong>Record the CID:</strong> Every file on IPFS has a unique <strong>Content Identifier (CID)</strong>. Save this hash (e.g., <code>Qm...</code>) in a notes app; it is the &quot;address&quot; you will use to retrieve your file later.</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. Verify and Delete Local Files</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Test Retrieval:</strong> Before deleting anything, enter your CID into an IPFS gateway (like <code>ipfs.io/ipfs/[Your-CID]</code>) in your mobile browser to confirm the file is accessible from the network.</li>
<li><strong>Remove Local Originals:</strong> Once verified, you can delete the original files from your phone&#39;s gallery or file manager.</li>
</ul>
<h3>3. Manage IPFS Cache (If running a local node)</h3>
<p>If you are using a specialized app that runs a local IPFS node, the node itself may take up space by caching other people&#39;s data.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Run Garbage Collection:</strong> Use the app&#39;s settings to &quot;Run Garbage Collector.&quot; This deletes unpinned cached data to reclaim disk space.</li>
<li><strong>Set Storage Limits:</strong> Configure a &quot;StorageMax&quot; limit in your IPFS settings (e.g., 2GB) to prevent the node from consuming too much phone memory.</li>
</ul>
