Enabling Android Debug Bridge (ADB) before a phone&#39;s initial setup is completed is generally restricted for security reasons. On most modern devices (Android 11 and later), standard ADB commands will not function until the setup wizard is finished and <strong>USB Debugging</strong> is manually enabled in Developer Options.</p>
<p>However, depending on your device and purpose, there are some advanced or specific methods to interact with a phone before setup is complete.</p>
<h3>1. Bypassing Setup Wizard via ADB (If Enabled)</h3>
<p>If your device&#39;s firmware allows ADB connections by default (rare on stock retail devices but common on some enterprise or development units), you can attempt to skip the setup screens using these shell commands once connected to a PC:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>adb shell settings put global setup_wizard_has_run 1</code></li>
<li><code>adb shell settings put secure user_setup_complete 1</code></li>
<li><code>adb shell settings put global device_provisioned 1</code></li>
<li><code>adb reboot</code></li>
</ul>
<h3>2. Specialized OEM/Secret Codes</h3>
<p>Some manufacturers include &quot;hidden&quot; menus accessible from the initial &quot;Welcome&quot; or &quot;Emergency Call&quot; screens that can trigger ADB or bypass setup:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Samsung:</strong> Some models may allow entering a dialer code (like <code>*#0*#</code>) to open a test menu, which sometimes enables an ADB interface that can be used for factory resets or FRP bypasses.</li>
<li><strong>Oppo/Other OEMs:</strong> Certain accessibility settings or specific button combinations may allow &quot;cheat codes&quot; to reach settings and enable debugging.</li>
</ul>
<h3>3. Using Recovery Mode</h3>
<p>If the phone is inaccessible through the standard OS, you can still use some ADB-like functionality via recovery or fastboot:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>ADB Sideload:</strong> While in Recovery Mode, you can use <code>adb sideload &lt;filename&gt;.zip</code> to install official system updates or certain signed packages even if USB debugging is off in the main OS.</li>
<li><strong>Fastboot Mode:</strong> By booting into Fastboot (typically power + volume down), you can use <code>fastboot</code> commands to flash firmware or unlock bootloaders, which does not require the phone to be &quot;set up&quot;.</li>
</ul>
<h3>4. Hardware/Provisioning Methods</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>OTG Devices:</strong> For some enterprise-managed devices, plugging in a specific USB-OTG provisioning drive or scanning a QR code at the start of the setup wizard can trigger automated setup protocols.</li>
<li><strong>TWRP/Custom Recovery:</strong> If the device has an unlocked bootloader and a custom recovery like TWRP installed, ADB is typically enabled by default within that recovery environment, allowing you to modify system files (like <code>build.prop</code>) to force-enable ADB in the main OS.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Summary of Prerequisites</h3>
<p>For any of these methods to work on a standard retail device in 2025:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The device must be connected</strong> to a PC with the <a href="https://developer.android.com/tools/releases/platform-tools">Android SDK Platform-Tools</a> installed.</li>
<li><strong>Drivers</strong> for your specific phone must be correctly installed on your computer.</li>
<li><strong>Authorizing the PC:</strong> Even if you trigger ADB, you must still be able to tap &quot;Allow&quot; on the phone&#39;s RSA fingerprint prompt to establish the connection.</li>
</ul>
