<p><strong>Your prompt:</strong><br>
factoring</p>
<p><strong>Search&#39;s response:</strong><br>
In finance, <strong>factoring</strong> is a transaction where a business sells its unpaid invoices (accounts receivable) to a third party, known as a <strong>factor</strong>, at a discount in exchange for immediate cash. This practice helps businesses improve cash flow without taking on additional debt.</p>
<h3>How Factoring Works</h3>
<p>Factoring involves a business, its customer, and a factoring company. The business provides goods or services and issues an invoice. Instead of waiting for payment, the business sells the invoice to a factor at a discount. The factor provides an immediate cash advance (70-90% of the invoice value), collects the full payment from the customer, and then remits the remaining balance to the business, less fees (typically 1-5%). Customers are usually notified to pay the factor directly.</p>
<h3>Key Types of Factoring</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Recourse Factoring:</strong> The business is responsible if the customer doesn&#39;t pay.</li>
<li><strong>Non-Recourse Factoring:</strong> The factor takes on the risk of customer non-payment due to insolvency. This option is more expensive.</li>
<li><strong>Spot Factoring:</strong> Allows businesses to factor individual invoices as needed without a contract.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Pros and Cons for Businesses</h3>

























<table><thead><tr><th align="left">Advantages</th><th align="left">Disadvantages</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left">Immediate cash flow.</td><td align="left">Higher costs than loans.</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Not debt.</td><td align="left">May affect customer relationships.</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Easier to qualify for than bank loans.</td><td align="left">Loss of control over collections.</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Outsourced collections.</td><td align="left">Not for B2C businesses.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>Factoring is most suitable for B2B companies, particularly startups or growing businesses in sectors like manufacturing and transportation, that need quick access to capital to manage expenses due to slow-paying customers.</p>
