You cannot configure <strong>100.64.0.7</strong> as a public or private DNS server on an Android device because that IP address belongs to a special-use, non-globally routable address space reserved for <strong>Carrier-Grade NAT (CGNAT)</strong> use by internet service providers (ISPs), not public DNS services. Android requires a valid, accessible DNS server address (like a public one or one on your local network) for the setting to work.</p>
<h3>Recommended Public DNS Servers</h3>
<p>Instead, you can use a reputable, public DNS server address. Common options include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Cloudflare DNS:</strong>
<ul>
<li>IPv4: <code>1.1.1.1</code> and <code>1.0.0.1</code></li>
<li>Private DNS Hostname: <code>one.one.one.one</code></li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Google Public DNS:</strong>
<ul>
<li>IPv4: <code>8.8.8.8</code> and <code>8.8.4.4</code></li>
<li>Private DNS Hostname: <code>dns.google</code></li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Quad9:</strong>
<ul>
<li>IPv4: <code>9.9.9.9</code> and <code>149.112.112.112</code></li>
<li>Private DNS Hostname: <code>dns.quad9.net</code></li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<h3>How to Configure a Valid DNS on Android</h3>
<p>You have two main methods to configure a valid DNS on Android: using <strong>Private DNS</strong> (recommended for Android 9 and later) or setting a <strong>static IP for a Wi-Fi network</strong>.</p>
<h4>Method 1: Using Private DNS (Android 9+)</h4>
<p>This method encrypts your DNS queries (DNS-over-TLS) and applies to both Wi-Fi and mobile data.</p>
<ol>
<li>Open your Android device&#39;s <strong>Settings</strong>.</li>
<li>Tap on <strong>Network &amp; internet</strong> (or <strong>Connections</strong> &gt; <strong>More connection settings</strong> on some devices).</li>
<li>Scroll down and tap <strong>Private DNS</strong>.</li>
<li>Select the <strong>Private DNS provider hostname</strong> option.</li>
<li>Enter the hostname for your chosen provider (e.g., <code>dns.google</code> or <code>one.one.one.one</code>).</li>
<li>Tap <strong>Save</strong> to apply the settings.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Method 2: Setting Static IP and DNS for Wi-Fi (All Android versions)</h4>
<p>This only changes the DNS for the specific Wi-Fi network you are connected to.</p>
<ol>
<li>Go to <strong>Settings</strong> &gt; <strong>Network &amp; internet</strong> &gt; <strong>Wi-Fi</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Long-press</strong> on your connected Wi-Fi network name, then tap <strong>Modify network</strong> or <strong>Manage network settings</strong>.</li>
<li>Check <strong>Advanced options</strong> (if available) and in the <strong>IP settings</strong> section, switch from <strong>DHCP</strong> to <strong>Static</strong>.</li>
<li>Scroll down to find the fields for <strong>DNS 1</strong> and <strong>DNS 2</strong>.</li>
<li>Enter a valid public DNS address in the <strong>DNS 1</strong> field (e.g., <code>8.8.8.8</code>).</li>
<li>Enter a secondary DNS address in the <strong>DNS 2</strong> field (e.g., <code>8.8.4.4</code>).</li>
<li>Tap <strong>Save</strong>.</li>
</ol>
