how to find something 404&#39;d on github</p>
<p><strong>Search&#39;s response:</strong><br>
To find content that returns a 404 error on GitHub, you need to first understand <em>why</em> it&#39;s 404&#39;ing. The methods depend on whether the item was a file, a repository, or a GitHub Pages site, and if you are the owner or a visitor.</p>
<h3>For Repository Owners</h3>
<p>If you are the owner of the content, the 404 error typically means the file or repository was deleted, or there is a configuration issue (especially with GitHub Pages).</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Restoring a deleted repository:</strong> A deleted repository can often be restored within 90 days of deletion.
<ol>
<li>Click your profile picture in the top-right corner, then select <strong>Settings</strong>.</li>
<li>In the left sidebar, go to <strong>Repositories</strong> (under &quot;Code, planning, and automation&quot;).</li>
<li>Scroll down and click on <strong>Deleted repositories</strong>.</li>
<li>Next to the repository you want to restore, click <strong>Restore</strong>.</li>
</ol>
</li>
<li><strong>Recovering a deleted file:</strong>
<ol>
<li>Go to the commit history for the repository.</li>
<li>Find the commit where the file was deleted (the commit message usually indicates a deletion).</li>
<li>Click on the commit hash, and then browse the files as they were <em>before</em> that commit (often indicated by adding <code>~1</code> to the commit URL).</li>
<li>You can then view the raw file content and copy it into a new file in your current repository.</li>
</ol>
</li>
<li><strong>Troubleshooting GitHub Pages:</strong> Common causes for 404s on GitHub Pages include case-sensitivity issues in filenames (e.g., <code>Index.html</code> vs. <code>index.html</code>), incorrect branch settings, or a missing <code>index.html</code> file in the root directory. Check your repository&#39;s <strong>Settings</strong> -&gt; <strong>Pages</strong> section to verify the source and published URL.</li>
</ul>
<h3>For Visitors</h3>
<p>If you are a visitor trying to access content that now returns a 404, your options are more limited:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Check the Internet Archive (Wayback Machine):</strong> If the page was public and existed for some time, it might have been crawled and saved by the Wayback Machine.</li>
<li><strong>Look for a local clone:</strong> If you or someone you know had previously cloned the repository locally, the content might still be available on that machine.</li>
<li><strong>Contact the repository owner:</strong> The best option is often to reach out to the person or organization who owned the content and inform them of the broken link. They may be able to restore it or provide an updated link.</li>
<li><strong>Check for typos:</strong> Simple errors in the URL can also cause a 404 error. Double-check the spelling and path.</li>
</ul>
<br></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--6-col mdl-typography--body-1 mdl-typography--text-right"></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col mdl-typography--caption"><b>Products:</b><br>&emsp;AI Mode<br><b>Why is this here?</b><br>&emsp;This activity was saved to your Google Account because the following settings were on:&nbsp;Search Services History.&nbsp;You can control these settings &nbsp;<a href="https://myaccount.google.com/activitycontrols">here</a>.</div></div></div><div class="outer-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col mdl-shadow--2dp"><div class="mdl-grid"><div class="header-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col"><p class="mdl-typography--title">AI Mode<br></p></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--6-col mdl-typography--body-1">Searched for black and white and looks two-dimensional cave<br>Sep 17, 2025, 9:59:16 PM EDT<br><p><strong>Your prompt:</strong><br>
