<p><strong>Search&#39;s response:</strong><br>
The provided document contains the results of a Complete Blood Count (CBC) with Auto Differential, collected on June 15, 2025. This test assesses various blood components.</p>
<p><strong>The results include:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>White Blood Cells (WBC) with Auto Differential:</strong> This section details the count of each type of WBC, which are crucial for fighting infections. The differential includes:
<ul>
<li>Neutrophils %: Percentage of neutrophils among all WBCs.</li>
<li>Lymphocytes %: Percentage of lymphocytes among all WBCs.</li>
<li>Monocytes %: Percentage of monocytes among all WBCs.</li>
<li>Eosinophils %: Percentage of eosinophils among all WBCs.</li>
<li>Basophils %: Percentage of basophils among all WBCs.</li>
<li>Immature Granulocyte %: Percentage of immature granulocytes.</li>
<li>Neutrophils Absolute: The absolute number of neutrophils per unit of blood volume.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Red Blood Cell Parameters:</strong> These tests assess various aspects of red blood cells, responsible for oxygen transport.
<ul>
<li>RBC (Red Blood Cell): The number of red blood cells.</li>
<li>Hemoglobin: The amount of oxygen-carrying protein in red blood cells.</li>
<li>Hematocrit: The percentage of blood volume made up of red blood cells.</li>
<li>MCV (Mean Corpuscular Volume): The average size of red blood cells.</li>
<li>MCH (Mean Corpuscular Hemoglobin): The average amount of hemoglobin in each red blood cell.</li>
<li>MCHC (Mean Corpuscular Hemoglobin Concentration): The concentration of hemoglobin in red blood cells.</li>
<li>RDW (Red Cell Distribution Width): Measures the variation in size of red blood cells.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Platelets:</strong> These are cell fragments involved in blood clotting.
<ul>
<li>Platelets: The total number of platelets.</li>
<li>MPV (Mean Platelet Volume): The average size of platelets.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Understanding Normal Ranges:</strong></p>
<p>The normal ranges provided for each component on the report are for comparison. However, these ranges can vary slightly between labs.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Elevated WBCs (Leukocytosis):</strong> A high WBC count can indicate an infection, inflammation, or other conditions.</li>
<li><strong>Low WBCs (Leukopenia):</strong> A low WBC count can make one more susceptible to infections and may be caused by conditions like autoimmune disorders or certain medications.</li>
<li><strong>Low RBCs, Hemoglobin, or Hematocrit:</strong> Lower than normal levels of these indicates anemia, which can have various causes, including nutrient deficiencies or blood loss.</li>
<li><strong>High RBCs (Erythrocytosis):</strong> An elevated red blood cell count may be a sign of a medical condition.</li>
<li><strong>Abnormal Platelet Count:</strong> High or low platelet counts can be a sign of various conditions or a side effect of medication.</li>
</ul>
<p>The &quot;Neutrophils Absolute&quot; value is an important measure of the immune system&#39;s ability to fight bacterial infections. The normal range provided is 2.00 - 7.00 K/mm3.</p>
<p><strong>If the result for absolute neutrophils is outside of this range, it could indicate:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Neutropenia (low neutrophil count):</strong> This can increase the risk of infection. It may be caused by conditions like infections or autoimmune diseases.</li>
<li><strong>Neutrophilia (high neutrophil count):</strong> This often indicates the body is fighting an infection or inflammation.</li>
</ul>
<p>A healthcare provider is the best resource for interpreting specific test results in the context of overall health and medical history. They can explain the results and determine if further evaluation or treatment is needed.</p>
