&lt;=10mg/dL<br>Jun 15, 2025, 11:44:32 PM EDT<br></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--6-col mdl-typography--body-1 mdl-typography--text-right"></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col mdl-typography--caption"><b>Products:</b><br>&emsp;AI Mode<br><b>Why is this here?</b><br>&emsp;This activity was saved to your Google Account because the following settings were on:&nbsp;Search Services History.&nbsp;You can control these settings &nbsp;<a href="https://myaccount.google.com/activitycontrols">here</a>.</div></div></div><div class="outer-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col mdl-shadow--2dp"><div class="mdl-grid"><div class="header-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col"><p class="mdl-typography--title">AI Mode<br></p></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--6-col mdl-typography--body-1">Searched for Foci of periorbital gas are noted without visible post septal extension.<br>Jun 15, 2025, 11:35:38 PM EDT<br><p><strong>Your prompt:</strong><br>
Foci of periorbital gas are noted without visible post septal extension.</p>
<p><strong>Search&#39;s response:</strong><br>
The phrase &quot;Foci of periorbital gas are noted without visible post septal extension&quot; refers to the presence of pockets of air in the tissues surrounding the eye (periorbital area). The phrase &quot;without visible post septal extension&quot; indicates that the air accumulation is localized to the area in front of the orbital septum (pre-septal) and has not spread behind it into the orbit itself. The orbital septum is a thin membrane that acts as a barrier between the eyelid and the deeper structures of the orbit.</p>
<p><strong>Meaning in a medical context:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Periorbital gas:</strong> This is also known as periorbital or orbital emphysema, which is the abnormal presence of air within the soft tissues around the eye.</li>
<li><strong>Foci:</strong>  This term suggests there are multiple areas or pockets where the air is located.</li>
<li><strong>Without visible post septal extension:</strong> This indicates that the air accumulation is limited to the eyelids and surrounding tissues in front of the orbital septum and has not extended deeper into the orbital cavity.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Causes of periorbital gas:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Trauma:</strong> Orbital fractures, which can lead to air entering the orbit from the paranasal sinuses.</li>
<li><strong>Postoperative complications:</strong> Following surgeries involving the face or eyes, such as eyelid surgery, nasal surgeries, or dental procedures.</li>
<li><strong>Infection:</strong>  In some cases, infections can lead to the formation of gas in the periorbital area.</li>
<li><strong>Increased pressure:</strong> Activities like forceful nose blowing, coughing, or sneezing can also force air into the periorbital tissues, particularly if there is a defect in the orbital wall.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Clinical significance:</strong></p>
<p>While orbital emphysema is generally benign and often resolves on its own, it can potentially lead to complications, such as:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Increased intraocular pressure:</strong>  The trapped air can exert pressure on the eye.</li>
<li><strong>Vision changes:</strong> Including decreased vision or double vision.</li>
<li><strong>Orbital compartment syndrome:</strong> In rare and severe cases, the pressure build-up can compress structures like the optic nerve and potentially lead to vision loss.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Treatment:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Observation:</strong>  In mild cases, simply observing the condition is sufficient as it usually resolves spontaneously.</li>
<li><strong>Antibiotics:</strong> If the emphysema is associated with an orbital fracture and there&#39;s a risk of infection from the sinuses.</li>
<li><strong>Decompression:</strong> In cases where vision is threatened due to high intraocular pressure, removal of the trapped air through needle aspiration or surgical decompression may be necessary.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>In summary,</strong> the presence of periorbital gas without post septal extension suggests a localized accumulation of air in the eyelids and surrounding tissues in front of the orbital septum.  While typically a benign condition, it is important to identify and monitor for potential complications.  Treatment depends on the severity and underlying cause.</p>
